#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
nyxus-hemera — the scan engine behind Settings ▸ Security (HEMERA).

★ WHY THIS EXISTS, and the one rule it is built around.

  The owner approved the Hemera scan experience on 2026-08-15 (`WIP-654`):
  a Quick Scan, a weekly full scan that stays out of the way, three volumes
  of notification, a protection history, right-click "Scan with Hemera" —
  and, at the centre of all of it, a QUARANTINE FLOW THAT NEVER DELETES.

  A finding is ISOLATED into a locked vault stripped of its exec bits, and
  then the machine STOPS and asks. Restore, Delete or Keep is the person's
  call, every time, with no timeout and no default. An antivirus that
  deletes the wedding photos it mis-scored is worse than no antivirus at
  all, and this build's whole premise is a machine a non-expert can trust.

  So: the ONLY code in this file that can remove a file the person owns is
  `_drop_original_after_verify`, which refuses unless the vault copy is
  proven byte-for-byte first, and `cmd_vault_delete` / `cmd_vault_restore`,
  which exist only because the person typed the word. Gate 13q29 pins that
  set by parsing this file's AST — a new deletion anywhere else fails the
  build, not a review.

⛔ DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, by design and not omission (`WIP-654`):

  · NO real-time on-access daemon. `clamd`/`clamonacc` are never started,
    never enabled, never referenced. Scans happen when the person asks or
    when the weekly timer fires, and nothing sits in the kernel's way of
    every open(2).
  · NO imports from the NYXUS security stack. Suxyn was forked from NYXUS
    on 2026-08-02 with that stack stripped ON PURPOSE. This file reaches
    `~/Nyxus-Core` for nothing, ever. The fork boundary is not a suggestion.

⛔ NO TERMINAL, EVER — the owner's absolute rule. Everything here is either
   a timer, a context-menu entry, or a Settings page calling `--json`.

★ AND THE SECOND RULE, added 2026-08-15 (`WIP-665`): THE DOWNLOAD IS ASKED FOR.

  clamav ships with NO definitions. Nothing in this build ever ran the
  downloader, so on a real machine every scan honestly reported "no virus
  definitions downloaded yet" and Hemera could not find anything at all.

  The owner ruled on 2026-08-15, in these terms: a PROMPTED FIRST-BOOT
  DOWNLOAD. Not bundled into the ISO — the image stays its current size.
  Not a silent background fetch — nobody is surprised by ~300 MB moving on
  their connection. The person is ASKED, once: what it is, roughly how big,
  and that scanning cannot work until it happens. Accept and it downloads;
  decline and the honest no-definitions state simply persists, visible, and
  can be accepted later from Settings ▸ Security.

  So `_fetch_definitions` — the only code in this build that downloads
  anything — is unreachable except through `cmd_defs_update`, which RETURNS
  before it on every path where consent was not given. Gate 13q31 pins that
  as wiring, the same way 13q29 pins the no-auto-delete promise.

★ AND THE LIE THIS FILE MUST NEVER TELL: "you are fine" because it is empty.

  A security tool that reports a clean result when it had nothing to scan
  with is worse than no security tool, because the person stops looking.
  "No definitions" is therefore its own outcome at EVERY layer — its own
  exit code, its own `result` in the JSON, its own history event, and its
  own notification — and it is never, anywhere, folded into "clean".

SURFACES
    nyxus-hemera scan quick            Downloads · Desktop · temp
    nyxus-hemera scan full             the whole home directory, low priority
    nyxus-hemera scan path P [P...]    one file or folder (the right-click)
    nyxus-hemera vault list [--json]   what is isolated, and what state it is in
    nyxus-hemera vault restore ID      put it back where it came from
    nyxus-hemera vault delete ID       destroy it — the person's word, never ours
    nyxus-hemera vault keep ID         leave it locked away, mark it reviewed
    nyxus-hemera history [--json] [--limit N]
    nyxus-hemera defs [--json]         definition freshness
    nyxus-hemera defs prompt [--json]  ASK once whether to download them
    nyxus-hemera defs update --consent [--json]
                                       download them — refuses without consent
    nyxus-hemera status [--json]       one summary for the Settings page

EXIT CODES — because a timer and a menu entry see only this number
    0  clean, or the command did what it said
    1  a scan found something (clamscan's own code, kept)
    2  usage: nothing to scan, no such vault item
    3  the scan could not run — the scanner itself failed
    4  THE SCAN DID NOT RUN BECAUSE THERE ARE NO DEFINITIONS. Distinct from
       3 on purpose: one means "the tool is broken", the other means "the
       tool is empty and here is the cure". Neither is ever 0.
    5  a download was refused because nobody had consented to it
    6  a consented download did not produce definitions

THREE VOLUMES (the owner's words: quiet on start, normal on a clean finish,
CRITICAL on a finding)
    start           urgency low      — a whisper, easy to miss on purpose
    clean complete  urgency normal   — "nothing found", said once
    finding         urgency critical — with a Review button that opens
                                       Settings ▸ Security

WHERE THINGS LIVE (all under the person's own home — nothing system-wide)
    ~/.local/share/nyxus/hemera/vault/        the locked vault, 0700
    ~/.local/share/nyxus/hemera/history.jsonl the protection history
    ~/.cache/nyxus/hemera-state.json          last-scan summary for the page
  `NYXUS_HEMERA_HOME` relocates all three. It exists so the proof harness can
  run the REAL engine against a throwaway tree instead of a mock — the same
  confined-HOME pattern the Settings proofs use.

© 2026 JOSEPH A. SIERENGOWSKI · NYX-J5W-2026-SIERENGOWSKI-LOCKED
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path

APP_NAME = "Hemera"

# ── the vault's two permissions, and why they are constants ──────────────
# They are named, module-level and asserted by gate 13q29 because "stripped
# of exec permissions" is the row's own words and a literal buried in a
# function is a thing that drifts. 0o600: readable and writable by the owner
# and by nobody else, and NO exec bit on any of the three triads. 0o700 on
# the directory: a directory needs its own +x to be traversed at all, so the
# lock there is the group/other zeros, not the owner's x.
VAULT_BLOB_MODE = 0o600
VAULT_DIR_MODE = 0o700
VAULT_META_MODE = 0o600

# Scan kinds, and what each one looks at.
KIND_QUICK = "quick"
KIND_FULL = "full"
KIND_PATH = "path"

# clamscan's own exit codes.
CLAM_CLEAN = 0
CLAM_FOUND = 1

# ── exit codes that are NOT clamscan's, and why they are constants ────────
# The Settings page reads `--json`; a systemd unit, a context-menu entry and
# any shell see only this number. So the number has to carry the one
# distinction that matters most in this file: "I looked and found nothing"
# and "I had nothing to look WITH" are different answers, and only the first
# one is 0.
#
# ⚠ cmd_scan's existing `return 3` and `return 2` are deliberately left as
#   literals. Gate 13q29 check 7 asserts the hard-failure branch as an `If`
#   testing `scanned` that contains a literal Constant 3; swapping that for a
#   name would blind a gate that is doing its job. New codes get constants;
#   the ones an existing gate already pins do not move.
EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_NO_DEFINITIONS = 4
EXIT_NO_CONSENT = 5
EXIT_UPDATE_FAILED = 6

# The `result` a scan records when it stopped because it had no signatures.
# It is its own word — never "clean", and never folded into "error" either,
# because "the tool is empty" has a cure the person can act on and "the tool
# is broken" does not.
RESULT_NO_DEFS = "no-definitions"


class HemeraUnsafe(RuntimeError):
    """Raised when an invariant that protects the person's file does not hold.

    Every raise of this ends with the original file still exactly where the
    person left it. That is the entire point of the class.
    """


# ── where things live ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def hemera_home() -> Path:
    override = os.environ.get("NYXUS_HEMERA_HOME")
    if override:
        return Path(override)
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_DATA_HOME") or (Path.home() / ".local" / "share")
    return Path(base) / "nyxus" / "hemera"


def vault_dir() -> Path:
    return hemera_home() / "vault"


def history_path() -> Path:
    return hemera_home() / "history.jsonl"


def state_path() -> Path:
    override = os.environ.get("NYXUS_HEMERA_HOME")
    if override:
        return Path(override) / "state.json"
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_CACHE_HOME") or (Path.home() / ".cache")
    return Path(base) / "nyxus" / "hemera-state.json"


def ensure_vault() -> Path:
    v = vault_dir()
    v.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    os.chmod(v, VAULT_DIR_MODE)
    return v


# ── the history log ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def log_event(event: str, **fields) -> None:
    """Append one line to the protection history.

    JSON Lines on purpose: appending cannot corrupt what is already written,
    a truncated tail costs one row instead of the file, and the Settings page
    reads it with two lines of Python. Never rewritten, never rotated in
    place — see `history --limit`.
    """
    row = {"ts": int(time.time()), "event": event}
    row.update(fields)
    try:
        p = history_path()
        p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with p.open("a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(json.dumps(row, sort_keys=True) + "\n")
    except OSError:
        # A history that cannot be written must never take a scan down with
        # it. The scan is the protection; the log is the receipt.
        pass


def read_history(limit: int = 200) -> list[dict]:
    try:
        lines = history_path().read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return []
    out: list[dict] = []
    for line in lines[-limit:] if limit > 0 else lines:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            out.append(json.loads(line))
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return out


# ── notifications, at exactly three volumes ──────────────────────────────

def notify(urgency: str, title: str, body: str, *,
           icon: str = "security-medium", review: bool = False,
           quiet: bool = False) -> None:
    if quiet or not shutil.which("notify-send"):
        return
    cmd = ["notify-send", "--app-name", APP_NAME,
           "--urgency", urgency, "--icon", icon]
    if review:
        # `-A` implies --wait: notify-send stays alive until the person
        # dismisses it or clicks, and prints the action key. That is what
        # makes the button work without this process owning a DBus loop —
        # nyxus-update-check established the pattern and the unit gives us
        # the timeout to match.
        cmd += ["-A", "review=Review"]
    cmd += [title, body]
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
                           timeout=24 * 60 * 60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return
    if review and p.stdout.strip() == "review":
        open_security_page()


def notify_ask(urgency: str, title: str, body: str,
               actions: list[tuple[str, str]], *,
               icon: str = "security-medium", quiet: bool = False) -> str:
    """Ask a question on the desktop and return the key the person chose.

    ⛔ AN EMPTY ANSWER IS NOT A YES.
       If there is no notification daemon, if the notice is swiped away, if
       the process is killed — this returns "". The only thing silence is
       ever allowed to do in this file is nothing at all. Every caller treats
       "" as "did not answer", which is neither consent nor refusal.

    `notify-send -A key=Label` implies --wait: it stays alive until the
    person clicks or dismisses, then prints the chosen key. That is how the
    buttons work without this script owning a DBus main loop — the same
    mechanism `notify()`'s Review button uses, and the reason the units that
    run these commands carry `TimeoutStartSec=infinity`.
    """
    if quiet or not shutil.which("notify-send"):
        return ""
    cmd = ["notify-send", "--app-name", APP_NAME,
           "--urgency", urgency, "--icon", icon]
    for key, label in actions:
        cmd += ["-A", f"{key}={label}"]
    cmd += [title, body]
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
                           timeout=24 * 60 * 60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return ""
    return p.stdout.strip()


def open_security_page() -> None:
    """Land on Settings ▸ Security — the Hemera page, never a terminal."""
    exe = shutil.which("nyxus-settings")
    if not exe:
        return
    try:
        subprocess.Popen([exe, "security"], start_new_session=True,
                         stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    except OSError:
        pass


# ── definition freshness ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

CLAM_DB_DIRS = ("/var/lib/clamav",)

# Only `.cvd` and `.cld` count, and that is not laziness. Those are the two
# forms a real ClamAV signature database takes — the signed container and its
# incrementally-updated cousin. A `.hdb` written by hand (which is what this
# build's own proof harness feeds the scanner) is a signature file, not a
# database the person can rely on, and counting one would let a test double
# masquerade as a protected machine.
CLAM_DB_SUFFIXES = (".cvd", ".cld")


def clam_db_dirs() -> tuple[str, ...]:
    """Where the signature databases live.

    `NYXUS_HEMERA_DB_DIR` relocates the check, and it exists for exactly one
    reason: the proof harness has to put this engine in BOTH states — with
    definitions and without — and the only honest way to do that without a
    network call is to point the check somewhere else. On a real machine
    this is `/var/lib/clamav` and nothing else. Same confined-tree pattern as
    `NYXUS_HEMERA_HOME`.
    """
    override = os.environ.get("NYXUS_HEMERA_DB_DIR")
    return (override,) if override else CLAM_DB_DIRS


# ── the record of what the person decided about the download ─────────────
#
# It lives beside the vault rather than in the cache, and that is deliberate:
# a cache is a thing you are allowed to throw away, and throwing this away
# would ask somebody a question they have already answered.

def defs_state_path() -> Path:
    return hemera_home() / "definitions.json"


def load_defs_state() -> dict:
    try:
        d = json.loads(defs_state_path().read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return d if isinstance(d, dict) else {}


def save_defs_state(data: dict) -> None:
    try:
        p = defs_state_path()
        p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        p.write_text(json.dumps(data, sort_keys=True), encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        # Losing this record costs the person one repeated question. Taking a
        # scan or a download down over it would cost them the feature.
        pass


def defs_info() -> dict:
    """Age of the newest ClamAV database, or an honest 'never'.

    ClamAV ships with NO definitions — they are downloaded after install. A
    brand-new machine that has never been online therefore has a scanner and
    nothing to scan WITH, and saying "0 days old" there would be a lie the
    person acts on. `days` stays None and `present` stays False.

    ⚠ THE SHAPE OF THIS DICT IS A CONTRACT. `defs --json` is what the
      Settings ▸ Security page renders, so `present`, `days`, `files`,
      `newest` and `note` keep their names and their meanings. `prompt` and
      `last_update` were ADDED on 2026-08-15 (`WIP-665`) so the page can say
      "you chose Not now on the 15th" instead of only "no definitions" —
      additive, never a rename. Gate 13q31 pins all five original keys.
    """
    newest = 0.0
    count = 0
    for d in clam_db_dirs():
        try:
            for p in Path(d).iterdir():
                if p.suffix in CLAM_DB_SUFFIXES:
                    count += 1
                    newest = max(newest, p.stat().st_mtime)
        except OSError:
            continue
    st = load_defs_state()
    decided = {"prompt": st.get("prompt") or {},
               "last_update": st.get("last_update") or {}}
    if count == 0:
        return {"present": False, "days": None, "files": 0, "newest": None,
                "note": "no virus definitions downloaded yet", **decided}
    days = max(0, int((time.time() - newest) // 86400))
    return {"present": True, "days": days, "files": count,
            "newest": int(newest),
            "note": f"definitions {days} day(s) old", **decided}


# ── the download, and the consent that is the only way to reach it ───────

DEFS_DOWNLOADER = "freshclam"
# The size the person is told before anything moves. It is the figure the
# owner ruled on and it has never been measured here, because this build has
# never run the real download — see the brief's unproven list, which says so
# in those words.
DEFS_SIZE_NOTE = "about 300 MB"
DEFS_FETCH_TIMEOUT = 60 * 60
DEFS_ASK_TITLE = "Hemera has no virus definitions yet"
DEFS_ASK_BODY = (
    "Scanning cannot find anything until the virus definitions are "
    f"downloaded — {DEFS_SIZE_NOTE}, once. Download them now?")


def _fetch_definitions() -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Run the downloader. THE ONLY CODE IN THIS BUILD THAT DOWNLOADS ANYTHING.

    It is deliberately stupid. It decides nothing, asks nobody, and reads no
    state — it takes the person's connection, uses it, and reports what
    happened. Everything that could possibly be a decision lives in
    `cmd_defs_update`, which is the only caller, and which returns before
    reaching this function on every path where consent was not given.

    Root is required because the databases live in a system directory, so a
    normal session goes through `pkexec` — the same route Settings ▸ Security
    already uses for its privileged actions, and a route that shows the
    person a dialog they can cancel. Cancelling is a refusal, not an error.
    """
    exe = shutil.which(DEFS_DOWNLOADER)
    if not exe:
        return 127, f"{DEFS_DOWNLOADER} is not installed"
    cmd = [exe, "--stdout"]
    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        pk = shutil.which("pkexec")
        if not pk:
            return 126, "there is no way to ask for administrator approval"
        cmd = [pk, exe, "--stdout"]
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
                           timeout=DEFS_FETCH_TIMEOUT)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        return 124, "the download did not finish in time"
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return 125, f"the downloader could not be started ({exc})"
    return p.returncode, (p.stdout + "\n" + p.stderr).strip()


def _fetch_failure_reason(rc: int, output: str) -> str:
    """Say what went wrong in words the person can act on.

    Every branch here is a real failure of a real first boot: a laptop with
    no network yet, a mirror having a bad day, a cancelled password dialog,
    a disk with no room for 300 MB. None of them may ever be reported as a
    success, and none of them are worth a CRITICAL notification either —
    a definition download that did not happen is an inconvenience, not a
    threat, and treating it as an alarm is how people learn to ignore alarms.
    """
    low = output.lower()
    if rc == 127:
        return "the definition downloader is not installed"
    if rc == 126:
        return "this session cannot ask for administrator approval"
    if rc == 124:
        return "the download did not finish in time"
    if rc == 125:
        return "the downloader could not be started"
    if any(k in low for k in ("not authorized", "authentication",
                              "dismissed", "authorization")):
        return "administrator approval was not given"
    if any(k in low for k in ("resolve", "getaddrinfo", "connect",
                              "network is unreachable", "no route",
                              "temporary failure", "timed out")):
        return "there is no network connection right now"
    if any(k in low for k in ("404", "not found", "mirror", "http")):
        return "the definition mirror did not answer"
    if any(k in low for k in ("no space", "disk full", "write")):
        return "there was not enough room to save the definitions"
    if any(k in low for k in ("permission denied", "can't create",
                              "can't open")):
        return "the definitions directory could not be written"
    return "the download did not complete"


def cmd_defs_update(*, consent: bool, as_json: bool = False,
                    quiet: bool = False, source: str = "settings") -> int:
    """Download the definitions — ONLY because somebody said yes.

    ⛔ THE GUARD BELOW IS THE WHOLE FEATURE, not a formality.

       The owner ruled on 2026-08-15 that this download is PROMPTED. So
       `consent` is not a convenience argument; it is the record that a human
       was asked and answered yes, and the `return` under it happens BEFORE
       `_fetch_definitions` is reached on every path through this function.
       Gate 13q31 asserts exactly that as an ORDER — the guard's return line
       precedes the first fetch call — so a break that leaves every string in
       place and only removes the guard still fails the build.

    ⛔ AND SUCCESS IS MEASURED, NOT REPORTED. The downloader's exit code is
       not the answer to "does this machine have definitions now"; the
       definitions directory is. A mirror that returns a 200 and a truncated
       file, an interrupted transfer, a downloader that exits 0 having
       written nothing — all of them are failures here, because `after` is
       read back off the disk and both conditions have to hold.
    """
    if not consent:
        log_event("defs-update-refused", source=source,
                  reason="no consent was recorded")
        if as_json:
            print(json.dumps({"ok": False, "consent": False,
                              "reason": "no consent was recorded — nothing "
                                        "was downloaded",
                              "definitions": defs_info()},
                             indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        else:
            print("nyxus-hemera: refusing to download anything without the "
                  "person's consent — ask first with `defs prompt`, or pass "
                  "--consent from a surface that has just asked",
                  file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_NO_CONSENT

    before = defs_info()
    started = int(time.time())
    log_event("defs-update-start", source=source,
              had_definitions=bool(before["present"]))
    notify("low", "Downloading virus definitions",
           f"This is {DEFS_SIZE_NOTE} and happens once. You can keep working.",
           icon="security-medium", quiet=quiet)

    rc, output = _fetch_definitions()
    after = defs_info()
    ok = bool(after["present"]) and rc == 0
    if rc == 0 and not after["present"]:
        reason = ("the download reported success but no definitions arrived — "
                  "nothing was saved")
    else:
        reason = "" if ok else _fetch_failure_reason(rc, output)

    state = load_defs_state()
    prompt = dict(state.get("prompt") or {})
    prompt.setdefault("asked_at", started)
    prompt["answer"] = "accepted"
    prompt["answered_at"] = started
    state["prompt"] = prompt
    state["last_update"] = {
        "started": started, "finished": int(time.time()), "ok": ok,
        "rc": rc, "source": source, "reason": reason,
        "files_before": before["files"], "files_after": after["files"]}
    save_defs_state(state)
    log_event("defs-update-complete", source=source, ok=ok, rc=rc,
              reason=reason, files=after["files"])

    if ok:
        notify("normal", "Virus definitions are ready",
               f"Hemera can scan now — {after['files']} definition file(s) "
               f"downloaded.", icon="security-high", quiet=quiet)
    else:
        # Normal volume, said once, with no retry loop behind it. The person
        # can try again from Settings ▸ Security whenever they like.
        notify("normal", "Could not download virus definitions",
               f"{reason.capitalize()}. Hemera still cannot scan. You can try "
               f"again from Settings ▸ Security.",
               icon="security-low", quiet=quiet)

    result = {"ok": ok, "consent": True, "rc": rc, "source": source,
              "reason": reason, "definitions": defs_info()}
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(result, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
    elif ok:
        print(f"definitions downloaded: {after['files']} file(s)")
    else:
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: definitions were NOT downloaded — {reason}",
              file=sys.stderr)
    return EXIT_OK if ok else EXIT_UPDATE_FAILED


def cmd_defs_prompt(as_json: bool = False, quiet: bool = False) -> int:
    """Ask, once, whether to download the definitions.

    ⛔ ONCE. EVER. The answer is written down before anything else happens,
       and any recorded answer — yes, no, or a notice that was swiped away —
       makes every later run of this command a no-op. That is what stops a
       timer that fires on every boot from turning into a machine that nags,
       and it is asserted by gate 13q31 as an early return, not as a comment.

    ⛔ AND A DECLINE DOWNLOADS NOTHING. The only call to `cmd_defs_update`
       from here sits inside a branch testing the person's own literal
       choice, which is the same shape the delete path has carried since
       `WIP-654`: the machine acts on the word it was given, not on a
       default and not on a fallthrough.
    """
    d = defs_info()
    state = load_defs_state()
    prompt = dict(state.get("prompt") or {})

    def report(payload: dict, line: str) -> int:
        if as_json:
            print(json.dumps(payload, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        else:
            print(line)
        return EXIT_OK

    if d["present"]:
        return report({"asked": False, "answer": prompt.get("answer", ""),
                       "skipped": "definitions are already present",
                       "definitions": d},
                      "definitions are already present — nothing to ask")
    if prompt.get("answer"):
        return report({"asked": False, "answer": prompt["answer"],
                       "skipped": "this machine has already been asked once",
                       "definitions": d},
                      f"already asked once — the answer was "
                      f"{prompt['answer']}; nothing was downloaded")

    asked_at = int(time.time())
    answer = notify_ask("normal", DEFS_ASK_TITLE, DEFS_ASK_BODY,
                        [("download", "Download now"), ("later", "Not now")],
                        icon="security-low", quiet=quiet)
    prompt["asked_at"] = asked_at
    prompt["answered_at"] = int(time.time())
    prompt["answer"] = ("accepted" if answer == "download"
                        else "declined" if answer == "later"
                        else "dismissed")
    state["prompt"] = prompt
    save_defs_state(state)
    log_event("defs-prompt", answer=prompt["answer"])

    if answer == "download":
        return cmd_defs_update(consent=True, as_json=as_json, quiet=quiet,
                               source="first-boot prompt")

    # Declined, or never answered. NOTHING is downloaded, the honest
    # no-definitions state persists exactly as it was, and Settings ▸
    # Security still carries the button for the day they change their mind.
    return report({"asked": True, "answer": prompt["answer"],
                   "downloaded": False,
                   "note": "nothing was downloaded; definitions can still be "
                           "fetched from Settings ▸ Security",
                   "definitions": defs_info()},
                  f"asked once — {prompt['answer']}; nothing was downloaded")


# ── the vault ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def sha256_of(path: Path) -> str:
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    with path.open("rb") as fh:
        for chunk in iter(lambda: fh.read(1024 * 1024), b""):
            h.update(chunk)
    return h.hexdigest()


def _new_id(src: Path) -> str:
    stamp = datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%S")
    tag = hashlib.sha256(
        f"{src}{time.time_ns()}".encode("utf-8", "replace")).hexdigest()[:8]
    return f"{stamp}-{tag}"


def meta_path(item_id: str) -> Path:
    return vault_dir() / f"{item_id}.json"


def blob_path(item_id: str) -> Path:
    return vault_dir() / f"{item_id}.blob"


def read_meta(item_id: str) -> dict | None:
    try:
        return json.loads(meta_path(item_id).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


def write_meta(item_id: str, meta: dict) -> None:
    p = meta_path(item_id)
    p.write_text(json.dumps(meta, indent=2, sort_keys=True), encoding="utf-8")
    os.chmod(p, VAULT_META_MODE)


def list_vault() -> list[dict]:
    items: list[dict] = []
    try:
        names = sorted(p.stem for p in vault_dir().glob("*.json"))
    except OSError:
        return items
    for item_id in names:
        m = read_meta(item_id)
        if m:
            items.append(m)
    return items


def _write_blob(src: Path, blob: Path) -> None:
    """Copy the bytes into the vault, born with no exec bit and no group/other.

    O_EXCL so a colliding id can never silently overwrite an existing
    quarantine record, and the mode is passed to open(2) rather than chmod'd
    afterwards so the file is never, not even for a scheduling quantum,
    readable by anyone else.
    """
    fd = os.open(blob, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL, VAULT_BLOB_MODE)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "wb") as out, src.open("rb") as fh:
            shutil.copyfileobj(fh, out, length=1024 * 1024)
            out.flush()
            os.fsync(out.fileno())
    except BaseException:
        # A half-written blob is not a quarantine record; drop it and leave
        # the original alone. This removes only what THIS call created.
        _discard_partial_blob(blob)
        raise
    # Belt and braces against a permissive umask interaction or a filesystem
    # that ignores the open(2) mode: assert the state we need, do not assume
    # it. `_drop_original_after_verify` refuses if this ever failed.
    os.chmod(blob, VAULT_BLOB_MODE)


def _discard_partial_blob(blob: Path) -> None:
    """Remove a blob THIS process just created and could not finish.

    Deliberately its own function so the gate's pinned-set assertion reads as
    what it is: the only removals in this file are a failed copy of our own
    making, the verified drop of an original, and the two the person asked
    for by name.
    """
    try:
        os.unlink(blob)
    except OSError:
        pass


def _drop_original_after_verify(src: Path, blob: Path, digest: str,
                                size: int) -> None:
    """Remove the ORIGINAL — and ONLY after the vault copy is proven.

    ⛔ THIS IS THE ONLY PLACE IN THE SCAN PATH THAT REMOVES ANYTHING, and
       every one of these checks is a reason to walk away with the person's
       file untouched. If any of them raise, the caller records "could not
       isolate" and the file stays exactly where it was. Losing a file we
       could not safely copy is the failure this whole row exists to make
       impossible.
    """
    if not blob.exists():
        raise HemeraUnsafe("the vault copy does not exist")
    st = blob.stat()
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        raise HemeraUnsafe("the vault copy is not a regular file")
    if st.st_mode & 0o111:
        raise HemeraUnsafe("the vault copy still carries an exec bit")
    if st.st_mode & 0o077:
        raise HemeraUnsafe("the vault copy is reachable beyond its owner")
    if st.st_size != size:
        raise HemeraUnsafe("the vault copy is a different size than the original")
    if sha256_of(blob) != digest:
        raise HemeraUnsafe("the vault copy does not match the original's digest")
    os.unlink(src)


def quarantine(src: Path, signature: str, *, scan_id: str = "") -> dict:
    """Isolate one finding. Returns its vault record.

    The order is the whole safety argument: measure, copy, prove, and only
    then remove. Anything that goes wrong before the proof leaves the file
    on disk where the person put it.
    """
    src = Path(src)
    st = src.lstat()
    if stat.S_ISLNK(st.st_mode):
        raise HemeraUnsafe("refusing to quarantine a symlink")
    if not stat.S_ISREG(st.st_mode):
        raise HemeraUnsafe("refusing to quarantine a non-regular file")

    ensure_vault()
    item_id = _new_id(src)
    blob = blob_path(item_id)
    digest = sha256_of(src)
    size = st.st_size

    _write_blob(src, blob)
    try:
        _drop_original_after_verify(src, blob, digest, size)
    except (HemeraUnsafe, OSError):
        # We could not safely take custody, so we take none: the vault copy
        # goes and the person's file stays exactly where it was. An orphan
        # blob with no record beside it would be a file nobody can account
        # for, which is its own kind of data loss.
        _discard_partial_blob(blob)
        raise

    meta = {
        "id": item_id,
        "state": "quarantined",
        "origin": str(src),
        "origin_mode": stat.S_IMODE(st.st_mode),
        "size": size,
        "sha256": digest,
        "signature": signature,
        "quarantined_at": int(time.time()),
        "scan_id": scan_id,
        # Said in the record itself so a person reading the JSON, or the
        # Settings page rendering it, never has to infer the promise.
        "note": "isolated, exec permissions stripped — nothing is deleted "
                "until you choose Delete",
    }
    write_meta(item_id, meta)
    log_event("quarantine", id=item_id, origin=str(src),
              signature=signature, size=size, scan_id=scan_id)
    return meta


# ── scanning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def quick_targets() -> list[Path]:
    home = Path.home()
    cand = [home / "Downloads", home / "Desktop", Path("/tmp"),
            Path("/var/tmp")]
    return [p for p in cand if p.is_dir()]


def full_targets() -> list[Path]:
    return [Path.home()]


def _clam_excludes() -> list[str]:
    """Never scan our own vault.

    Left in, a full scan of the home directory re-detects every blob it
    isolated last week and quarantines the quarantine — a loop that grows
    the vault forever and reports the same threat every Sunday. clamscan
    takes a regex against the full path.
    """
    root = str(hemera_home()).replace("\\", "\\\\")
    for ch in ".^$*+?()[]{}|":
        root = root.replace(ch, "\\" + ch)
    return [f"--exclude-dir=^{root}"]


def run_clamscan(targets: list[Path]) -> tuple[int, list[tuple[Path, str]],
                                               int, int]:
    """(rc, [(path, signature)...], files_scanned, unreadable_items).

    rc is clamscan's own: 0 clean, 1 found, 2 error. A missing scanner or a
    missing database is an ERROR, never a clean result — reporting "no
    threats found" when nothing was actually examined is the single most
    dangerous lie this file could tell.

    `Total errors` is parsed for a reason measured on 2026-08-15: clamscan
    returns 2 for a WHOLE-RUN failure and ALSO for "I scanned everything I
    could and one item was unreadable". Verified against ClamAV 1.5.3 with a
    single mode-000 directory in the tree — rc 2, `Scanned files: 1`,
    `Total errors: 1`. A weekly scan of a real home directory hits an
    unreadable path constantly (fuse mounts, sockets, another user's file
    under /tmp), so treating every rc 2 as a dead scan would have made the
    scheduled scan report "scan did not run" essentially forever. The two
    cases are told apart by whether anything was actually examined.
    """
    exe = shutil.which("clamscan")
    if not exe:
        return 2, [], 0, 0
    cmd = [exe, "-r", "--infected", "--stdout", *_clam_excludes(),
           *[str(t) for t in targets]]
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return 2, [], 0, 0

    findings: list[tuple[Path, str]] = []
    scanned = 0
    errors = 0
    for line in p.stdout.splitlines():
        line = line.rstrip()
        if line.endswith(" FOUND"):
            body = line[: -len(" FOUND")]
            path_s, _, sig = body.rpartition(": ")
            if path_s:
                findings.append((Path(path_s), sig or "unknown"))
        elif line.startswith("Scanned files:"):
            try:
                scanned = int(line.split(":", 1)[1].strip())
            except ValueError:
                pass
        elif line.startswith("Total errors:"):
            try:
                errors = int(line.split(":", 1)[1].strip())
            except ValueError:
                pass
    return p.returncode, findings, scanned, errors


def save_state(data: dict) -> None:
    try:
        p = state_path()
        p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        p.write_text(json.dumps(data, sort_keys=True), encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass


def load_state() -> dict:
    try:
        return json.loads(state_path().read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def _no_definitions_stop(kind: str, *, quiet: bool, low: bool) -> int:
    """A scan that has nothing to scan WITH stops here, and says so everywhere.

    ⛔ WHY THIS RUNS BEFORE THE "Hemera is scanning" NOTICE.
       Whispering "scanning" and then finding out there are no signatures is
       a machine telling somebody it is protecting them while it is not. The
       cheap check comes first so the claim is never made. clamscan's own
       rc-2-with-nothing-scanned branch further down is the second line of
       defence and it stays exactly as `WIP-654` built it — that one catches
       a database that exists and is broken, which this one cannot see.

    ⛔ AND IT MUST NOT SPAM. `low` is the scheduled scan (the weekly timer's
       ExecStart is the only thing that passes it, and it means "nobody is
       sitting here waiting for this"). A timer that fires every Sunday must
       not deliver the same notice every Sunday, so the scheduled path says
       this ONCE in the machine's life — and not even once if the person was
       already asked and said no. An interactive scan always answers,
       because somebody just clicked something and is owed a reply.

       Quiet does not mean invisible: the history row and the recorded
       `result` are written on EVERY path, notification or not, and the exit
       code is non-zero, so the unit shows as failed rather than as a scan
       that went fine.
    """
    reason = "no virus definitions are downloaded yet"
    scan_id = datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%S")
    now = int(time.time())
    log_event("scan-skipped", scan_id=scan_id, kind=kind, reason=reason,
              scheduled=low)
    save_state({"scan_id": scan_id, "kind": kind, "started": now,
                "finished": now, "result": RESULT_NO_DEFS, "reason": reason,
                "files": 0, "findings": 0, "isolated": 0, "not_isolated": 0,
                "partial": False, "unreadable": 0})

    state = load_defs_state()
    declined = (state.get("prompt") or {}).get("answer") == "declined"
    already_told = bool(state.get("no_defs_notified_at"))
    body = ("There are no virus definitions on this machine yet, so nothing "
            "was checked. Download them from Settings ▸ Security.")
    if low:
        if not declined and not already_told:
            state["no_defs_notified_at"] = now
            save_defs_state(state)
            notify("normal", "Hemera could not scan", body,
                   icon="security-low", quiet=quiet)
    else:
        notify("normal", "Hemera could not scan", body,
               icon="security-low", review=True, quiet=quiet)

    print(f"nyxus-hemera: scan did not run — {reason}", file=sys.stderr)
    return EXIT_NO_DEFINITIONS


def cmd_scan(kind: str, paths: list[str], *, quiet: bool = False,
             low: bool = False) -> int:
    if low:
        # The unit already sets Nice= and IOSchedulingClass=idle; this is the
        # belt for the braces, and it also covers a scan started by hand.
        try:
            os.nice(19)
        except OSError:
            pass

    if kind == KIND_QUICK:
        targets, label = quick_targets(), "Downloads, Desktop and temporary files"
    elif kind == KIND_FULL:
        targets, label = full_targets(), "your whole home folder"
    else:
        targets = [Path(p) for p in paths]
        missing = [t for t in targets if not t.exists()]
        if missing or not targets:
            print(f"nyxus-hemera: nothing to scan: "
                  f"{', '.join(str(m) for m in missing) or '<no path given>'}",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        label = ", ".join(t.name or str(t) for t in targets)

    if not targets:
        print("nyxus-hemera: no scannable targets", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    # ⛔ NOTHING TO SCAN WITH — stop before this process claims to be
    #    scanning anything. `WIP-665`, and the return below is deliberately
    #    ahead of both the scan-start row and the "Hemera is scanning"
    #    notification; gate 13q31 asserts that order rather than the words.
    if not defs_info()["present"]:
        return _no_definitions_stop(kind, quiet=quiet, low=low)

    scan_id = datetime.now().strftime("%Y%m%dT%H%M%S")
    started = int(time.time())
    log_event("scan-start", scan_id=scan_id, kind=kind,
              targets=[str(t) for t in targets])
    # VOLUME 1 of 3 — quiet. A scan starting is not news.
    notify("low", "Hemera is scanning", f"Checking {label}.",
           icon="security-medium", quiet=quiet)

    rc, findings, scanned, errors = run_clamscan(targets)

    # clamscan's rc 2 covers two very different situations and telling them
    # apart is the difference between a feature that works on a real machine
    # and one that says "scan did not run" every single week:
    #
    #   · NOTHING was examined — no scanner, or no signature database. That
    #     is a genuine failure, and it must NEVER be reported as clean.
    #   · Something was examined and something else could not be read. The
    #     scan really ran; it just was not exhaustive, and saying exactly
    #     that is more useful than either "clean" or "failed".
    #
    # `scanned` is the discriminator, because it is the only number that
    # answers "did any file actually get looked at".
    partial = False
    if rc not in (CLAM_CLEAN, CLAM_FOUND):
        if scanned <= 0:
            d = defs_info()
            reason = ("no virus definitions are downloaded yet"
                      if not d["present"] else "the scanner could not run")
            log_event("scan-error", scan_id=scan_id, kind=kind, reason=reason)
            save_state({"scan_id": scan_id, "kind": kind, "started": started,
                        "finished": int(time.time()), "result": "error",
                        "reason": reason, "files": scanned, "findings": 0})
            print(f"nyxus-hemera: scan did not run — {reason}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 3
        partial = True

    isolated: list[dict] = []
    failed: list[str] = []
    for path, sig in findings:
        try:
            isolated.append(quarantine(path, sig, scan_id=scan_id))
        except (HemeraUnsafe, OSError) as exc:
            failed.append(str(path))
            log_event("quarantine-failed", scan_id=scan_id, origin=str(path),
                      signature=sig, reason=str(exc))

    finished = int(time.time())
    log_event("scan-complete", scan_id=scan_id, kind=kind, files=scanned,
              findings=len(findings), isolated=len(isolated),
              not_isolated=len(failed), partial=partial, unreadable=errors)
    save_state({"scan_id": scan_id, "kind": kind, "started": started,
                "finished": finished,
                "result": ("findings" if findings
                           else "partial" if partial else "clean"),
                "files": scanned, "findings": len(findings),
                "isolated": len(isolated), "not_isolated": len(failed),
                "partial": partial, "unreadable": errors})

    if not findings:
        # VOLUME 2 of 3 — normal. Said once, on a finish, never repeated.
        body = f"No threats found in {scanned} file(s)."
        if partial:
            # Never let "no threats found" stand alone when the scan could
            # not see everything. The count is real, so give the real count.
            body += (f" {errors} item(s) could not be read and were skipped."
                     if errors else " Some items could not be read.")
        notify("normal", "Scan complete", body,
               icon="security-high", quiet=quiet)
        print(f"{'partial' if partial else 'clean'}: {scanned} file(s) scanned")
        return 0

    # VOLUME 3 of 3 — CRITICAL, and it says what happened to the file in the
    # first sentence, because "threat found" without "and it is now in a
    # locked vault, nothing was deleted" is how people panic.
    n = len(findings)
    body = (f"{len(isolated)} moved to the locked vault — nothing was "
            f"deleted. Choose Restore, Delete or Keep.")
    if failed:
        body += f" {len(failed)} could not be isolated and were left in place."
    notify("critical", f"{n} threat{'s' if n != 1 else ''} found", body,
           icon="security-low", review=True, quiet=quiet)
    for m in isolated:
        print(f"quarantined: {m['id']}  {m['signature']}  {m['origin']}")
    for f in failed:
        print(f"NOT isolated (left in place): {f}", file=sys.stderr)
    return 1


# ── the three the person chooses between ─────────────────────────────────

def cmd_vault_restore(item_id: str) -> int:
    meta = read_meta(item_id)
    if not meta:
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: no vault item '{item_id}'", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if meta.get("state") != "quarantined" and meta.get("state") != "kept":
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: '{item_id}' is {meta.get('state')} — nothing to "
              f"restore", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    blob = blob_path(item_id)
    dest = Path(meta["origin"])
    if not blob.exists():
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: the vault copy of '{item_id}' is gone",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if dest.exists():
        # Refuse rather than clobber. Something now lives where this came
        # from, and overwriting it to undo a quarantine would destroy a file
        # nobody flagged.
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: {dest} exists again — refusing to overwrite it",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    try:
        dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copyfile(blob, dest)
        if sha256_of(dest) != meta["sha256"]:
            raise HemeraUnsafe("restored copy does not match the recorded digest")
        os.chmod(dest, meta.get("origin_mode", 0o600))
    except (HemeraUnsafe, OSError) as exc:
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: restore failed: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    # The bytes are back at the origin and verified; the vault copy is now a
    # duplicate. THIS removal is the person's own Restore, not ours.
    os.unlink(blob)
    meta["state"] = "restored"
    meta["restored_at"] = int(time.time())
    write_meta(item_id, meta)
    log_event("restore", id=item_id, origin=meta["origin"])
    print(f"restored: {dest}")
    return 0


def cmd_vault_delete(item_id: str) -> int:
    """Destroy a quarantined file — ONLY ever because the person said so.

    Nothing calls this on a timer, on a schedule, on a threshold or after N
    days. There is no such caller in this build and gate 13q29 fails if the
    scan path grows one.
    """
    meta = read_meta(item_id)
    if not meta:
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: no vault item '{item_id}'", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if meta.get("state") == "deleted":
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: '{item_id}' is already deleted", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    blob = blob_path(item_id)
    if blob.exists():
        os.unlink(blob)
    meta["state"] = "deleted"
    meta["deleted_at"] = int(time.time())
    write_meta(item_id, meta)
    log_event("delete", id=item_id, origin=meta.get("origin", ""))
    print(f"deleted: {item_id}")
    return 0


def cmd_vault_keep(item_id: str) -> int:
    meta = read_meta(item_id)
    if not meta:
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: no vault item '{item_id}'", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    if meta.get("state") not in ("quarantined", "kept"):
        print(f"nyxus-hemera: '{item_id}' is {meta.get('state')}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    meta["state"] = "kept"
    meta["kept_at"] = int(time.time())
    write_meta(item_id, meta)
    log_event("keep", id=item_id, origin=meta.get("origin", ""))
    print(f"kept in the vault: {item_id}")
    return 0


# ── read-only surfaces ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

def cmd_vault_list(as_json: bool) -> int:
    items = list_vault()
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(items, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        return 0
    if not items:
        print("the vault is empty")
        return 0
    for m in items:
        when = datetime.fromtimestamp(
            m.get("quarantined_at", 0)).strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
        print(f"{m['id']}  {m.get('state','?'):<11} {when}  "
              f"{m.get('signature','?')}  {m.get('origin','?')}")
    return 0


def cmd_history(as_json: bool, limit: int) -> int:
    rows = read_history(limit)
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(rows, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        return 0
    if not rows:
        print("no protection history yet")
        return 0
    for r in rows:
        when = datetime.fromtimestamp(r.get("ts", 0)).strftime("%Y-%m-%d %H:%M")
        detail = r.get("origin") or r.get("kind") or ""
        print(f"{when}  {r.get('event',''):<18} {detail}")
    return 0


def cmd_defs(as_json: bool) -> int:
    d = defs_info()
    print(json.dumps(d, indent=2, sort_keys=True) if as_json else d["note"])
    return 0


def cmd_status(as_json: bool) -> int:
    items = list_vault()
    counts: dict[str, int] = {}
    for m in items:
        counts[m.get("state", "?")] = counts.get(m.get("state", "?"), 0) + 1
    out = {
        "scanner": bool(shutil.which("clamscan")),
        "definitions": defs_info(),
        "last_scan": load_state(),
        "vault": {"total": len(items), "by_state": counts,
                  "awaiting_decision": counts.get("quarantined", 0)},
        # Stated in the machine-readable surface too, so the page cannot
        # paint a promise the engine does not keep.
        "auto_delete": False,
    }
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(out, indent=2, sort_keys=True))
        return 0
    ls = out["last_scan"]
    print(f"scanner installed: {'yes' if out['scanner'] else 'no'}")
    print(f"definitions: {out['definitions']['note']}")
    print(f"last scan: {ls.get('kind','never')} — {ls.get('result','n/a')}")
    print(f"vault: {out['vault']['total']} item(s), "
          f"{out['vault']['awaiting_decision']} awaiting your decision")
    return 0


# ── argv ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def build_parser() -> argparse.ArgumentParser:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="nyxus-hemera",
        description="Hemera — scan, isolate, and let the person decide.")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    sc = sub.add_parser("scan", help="scan for threats")
    sc.add_argument("kind", choices=[KIND_QUICK, KIND_FULL, KIND_PATH])
    sc.add_argument("paths", nargs="*", help="paths, for `scan path`")
    sc.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true",
                    help="send no desktop notifications")
    sc.add_argument("--low", action="store_true",
                    help="run at idle priority (the weekly timer sets this)")

    va = sub.add_parser("vault", help="what is isolated, and what to do with it")
    vsub = va.add_subparsers(dest="vcmd", required=True)
    vl = vsub.add_parser("list")
    vl.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    for name in ("restore", "delete", "keep"):
        vp = vsub.add_parser(name)
        vp.add_argument("id")

    hi = sub.add_parser("history", help="the protection history")
    hi.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    hi.add_argument("--limit", type=int, default=200)

    df = sub.add_parser("defs", help="virus definition freshness")
    df.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    # `defs` with no verb keeps reporting freshness exactly as it always has —
    # the Settings page reads that and the shape is a contract. The two verbs
    # are additive.
    dsub = df.add_subparsers(dest="dcmd")
    dup = dsub.add_parser(
        "update", help="download the definitions (needs the person's consent)")
    dup.add_argument("--json", dest="json_sub", action="store_true")
    dup.add_argument(
        "--consent", action="store_true",
        help="the person has just been asked and said yes. Without this "
             "nothing is downloaded — this flag IS the owner's 2026-08-15 "
             "ruling that the download is prompted, never silent")
    dup.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true",
                     help="send no desktop notifications")
    dup.add_argument("--source", default="settings",
                     help="which surface asked, for the history")
    dpr = dsub.add_parser(
        "prompt", help="ask ONCE whether to download the definitions")
    dpr.add_argument("--json", dest="json_sub", action="store_true")
    dpr.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true")

    st = sub.add_parser("status", help="one summary for the Settings page")
    st.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    return ap


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    args = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    if args.cmd == "scan":
        return cmd_scan(args.kind, args.paths, quiet=args.quiet, low=args.low)
    if args.cmd == "vault":
        if args.vcmd == "list":
            return cmd_vault_list(args.json)
        if args.vcmd == "restore":
            return cmd_vault_restore(args.id)
        if args.vcmd == "delete":
            return cmd_vault_delete(args.id)
        if args.vcmd == "keep":
            return cmd_vault_keep(args.id)
    if args.cmd == "history":
        return cmd_history(args.json, args.limit)
    if args.cmd == "defs":
        # `--json` is accepted on either side of the verb, so both halves are
        # read. argparse lets a subparser's own default overwrite the parent's
        # value when they share a dest, which is why they do not share one.
        as_json = bool(args.json) or bool(getattr(args, "json_sub", False))
        if args.dcmd == "update":
            # The person's own word, passed through verbatim. There is no
            # default here and there is no fallthrough: a caller that does
            # not say --consent gets a refusal, not a download.
            return cmd_defs_update(consent=args.consent, as_json=as_json,
                                   quiet=args.quiet, source=args.source)
        if args.dcmd == "prompt":
            return cmd_defs_prompt(as_json, quiet=args.quiet)
        return cmd_defs(as_json)
    if args.cmd == "status":
        return cmd_status(args.json)
    return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
